### Bluesky vs X? {#platform}
Let's look at last year's

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://www.kenkoonwong.com/blog/idweek25/index_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-12-1.png" alt="image" width="60%" height="auto">
</p>

And now this year's

```{r, echo=F,message=F,warning=F}
merge_clean |>
  mutate(date = date(created_at)) |>
  group_by(date, platform) |>
  summarize(n = n()) |>
  ggplot(aes(x=date,y=n,fill=platform)) +
  geom_col(alpha=0.5,color="black", position="dodge2") +
  theme_bw() +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("bluesky"="blue","x"="black")) +
  ggtitle("Bluesky vs X #IDweek2026 Posts Frequency")
```

We definitely see the difference. Even though day 1 bluesky had slightly more posts than X, but the rest of the days, X surpassed Bluesky, at least per visualization. Now, what if we perform the same statistical wizardry (for those who are interest: negative binomial regression to compare social media (bluesky vs X) engagement metrics between platforms across five dates, applying Benjamini-Hochberg correction for multiple testing with alpha 0.05) as last year and see if we notice any out of the norm difference between the engagements (e.g., likes, bookmarks, repost etc) and see if there is a difference between Bluesky and X.

```{r,echo=F,message=F,warning=F,eval=F}

## using wilcoxon, not correct tehcnique
merge_clean |>
  mutate(date = date(created_at)) |>
  group_by(date, platform) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c("quote_count":"bookmark_count"), names_to = "metric", values_to = "value") |>
  ggplot(aes(x=as.factor(date),y=value,fill=platform)) +
  # geom_boxplot(alpha=0.2) +
  # geom_point(alpha=0.2) +
  geom_violin(alpha=0.5) +
  ggpubr::stat_compare_means( aes(label = ifelse(..p.. < 0.002, paste0("p = ", ..p.format..), "ns")),
  size = 1.5, 
  method = "wilcox.test") +
  facet_wrap(.~metric, scales = "free_y") +
  theme_bw() +
  xlab("date") +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1, size = 8)) 

```


```{r,eval=T,echo=F, warning=F, message=F}
library(MASS)

load("idweek26_merge_hash.rda")

nbr_model_result <- tibble(date=as.Date(character()),metric=as.character(),coef=as.numeric(),pval=as.numeric())
dates <- c("10-20-26","10-21-26","10-22-26","10-23-26","10-24-26")
metric <- c("quote_count", "like_count", "reply_count", "repost_count", "bookmark_count")
# dates <- c("10-22-26")

### Test all rows
# for (i in dates) {
# 
# nbr_df <- merge_clean |>
#   mutate(date = date(created_at)) |>
#   filter(date == mdy(i))
# 
# for (j in metric) {
# # model
# model <- glm.nb(as.formula(paste(j, "~ platform")), data = nbr_df)
# pval <- summary(model)$coefficients[2,4]
# coef <- summary(model)$coefficients[2,1]
# 
# nbr_model_result <- nbr_model_result |>
#   bind_rows(tibble(date=mdy(i),metric=j,coef=coef,pval=pval))
# }
# }


## test only those posts posted same post in both platform
rep <- merge_clean |>
  group_by(post_hash) |>
  distinct(post_hash, text, .keep_all = T) |>
  mutate(row = row_number()) |>
  filter(row == 2) |>
  pull(post_hash)

# data_both <- merge_clean |>
#   filter(post_hash %in% rep)  

data_both <- merge_clean

for (i in dates) {

nbr_df <- data_both |>
  mutate(date = date(created_at)) |>
  filter(date == mdy(i))

for (j in metric) {
# model
if (sum(nbr_df[[j]])==0) { next }
model <- glm.nb(as.formula(paste(j, "~ platform")), data = nbr_df)
pval <- summary(model)$coefficients[2,4]
coef <- summary(model)$coefficients[2,1]

nbr_model_result <- nbr_model_result |>
  bind_rows(tibble(date=mdy(i),metric=j,coef=coef,pval=pval))
}
}




nbr_model_result <- nbr_model_result %>%
    mutate(pval_adj = p.adjust(pval, method = "BH"))

# First, prepare your plotting data
plot_data <- merge_clean %>%
  mutate(date = date(created_at)) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c("quote_count":"bookmark_count"), 
               names_to = "metric", 
               values_to = "value")

# Prepare annotation data from your NBR results
annotation_data <- nbr_model_result %>%
  # slice(1:25) |>
  mutate(
    label = case_when(
      pval_adj < 0.05 ~ paste0("p = ", round(pval_adj,5)),
      TRUE ~ "ns"
    ),
    date_factor = as.factor(date)
  ) %>%
  dplyr::select(date_factor, metric, coef, label, pval_adj)

# Create the plot
plot_data %>%
  ggplot(aes(x = as.factor(date), y = value, fill = platform)) +
  geom_violin(alpha = 0.5) +
  geom_text(data = annotation_data,
            aes(x = date_factor, y = Inf, label = label),
            inherit.aes = FALSE,
            vjust = 1.2,
            size = 1.5) +
  facet_wrap(. ~ metric, scales = "free_y") +
  theme_bw() +
  xlab("date") +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("bluesky"="blue","x"="red")) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1, size = 8))

# plot_data |> filter(date == mdy("10/22/26")) |> view()

```





```{r,echo=F, eval=T}
annotation_data2 <- annotation_data |>
  mutate(significant_BH = case_when(
    pval_adj < 0.05 ~ T,
    TRUE ~ F
  )) 

annotation_data2 |>   
  dplyr::select(-significant_BH,-label) |>
  kbl() %>%
  row_spec(which(annotation_data2$significant_BH), 
           background = "#90EE9050", bold = TRUE)
```

Wow, OK! X definitely has more bookmarks compared to Bluesky on Day 1 and 2 after FDR adjustment! I bet you this is probably highly influenced from `LordAlirezaF`'s contribution! Unlike last year where Bluesky was a clear winner, this year other than more bookmarks on X, there is no "winner"? 

Again, same as last year, the above people may say we're not being fair at comparing the engagement of some different contents (some posted on specific platform but not the other), why don't we only compare posts from authors who posted on both platform with similar technique as last year. 

Our result showed there were 133 duplicate posts. If we were to remove these duplicate posts and revisualize the counts on Bluesky and X.

```{r,echo=F,warning=F,message=F, eval=T}
data_both <- merge_clean |>
  filter(post_hash %in% rep)

merge_clean |>
  filter(!post_hash %in% rep) |>
  mutate(date = date(created_at)) |>
  group_by(date,platform) |>
  summarize(n = n()) |>
  ggplot(aes(x=date,y=n,fill=platform)) +
  geom_col(position="dodge2", alpha=0.5, color = "black") +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("bluesky"="blue","x"="black")) +
  theme_bw()
```

Alright, it looks quite similar to previous! So the duplicate didn't really make a whole lot of difference! 

Let's take a look whether there is a difference in engagement if we were to compare the SAME post in both platforms?

```{r, echo=F, eval=T, message=F, warning=F}
# First, prepare your plotting data

nbr_model_result <- tibble(date=as.Date(character()),metric=as.character(),coef=as.numeric(),pval=as.numeric())
dates <- c("10-20-26","10-21-26","10-22-26","10-23-26","10-24-26")
metric <- c("quote_count", "like_count", "reply_count", "repost_count", "bookmark_count")
# dates <- c("10-22-26")

### Test all rows
# for (i in dates) {
# 
# nbr_df <- merge_clean |>
#   mutate(date = date(created_at)) |>
#   filter(date == mdy(i))
# 
# for (j in metric) {
# # model
# model <- glm.nb(as.formula(paste(j, "~ platform")), data = nbr_df)
# pval <- summary(model)$coefficients[2,4]
# coef <- summary(model)$coefficients[2,1]
# 
# nbr_model_result <- nbr_model_result |>
#   bind_rows(tibble(date=mdy(i),metric=j,coef=coef,pval=pval))
# }
# }


## test only those posts posted same post in both platform
rep <- merge_clean |>
  group_by(post_hash) |>
  distinct(post_hash, text, .keep_all = T) |>
  mutate(row = row_number()) |> 
  filter(row == 2) |>
  pull(post_hash)

data_both <- merge_clean |>
  filter(post_hash %in% rep)


for (i in dates) {

nbr_df <- data_both |>
  mutate(date = date(created_at)) |>
  filter(date == mdy(i))

for (j in metric) {
# model
if (sum(nbr_df[[j]])==0) { next }
model <- glm.nb(as.formula(paste(j, "~ platform")), data = nbr_df)
pval <- summary(model)$coefficients[2,4]
coef <- summary(model)$coefficients[2,1]

nbr_model_result <- nbr_model_result |>
  bind_rows(tibble(date=mdy(i),metric=j,coef=coef,pval=pval))
}
}




nbr_model_result <- nbr_model_result %>%
    mutate(pval_adj = p.adjust(pval, method = "BH"))

plot_data <- data_both %>%
  mutate(date = date(created_at)) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c("quote_count":"bookmark_count"), 
               names_to = "metric", 
               values_to = "value")

# Prepare annotation data from your NBR results
annotation_data <- nbr_model_result %>%
  # slice(26:45) |>
  mutate(
    label = case_when(
      pval_adj < 0.05 ~ paste0("p = ", round(pval_adj,5)),
      TRUE ~ "ns"
    ),
    date_factor = as.factor(date)
  ) %>%
  dplyr::select(date_factor, metric, coef, label, pval_adj)

# Create the plot
plot_data %>%
  ggplot(aes(x = as.factor(date), y = value, fill = platform)) +
  geom_violin(alpha = 0.5) +
  geom_text(data = annotation_data,
            aes(x = date_factor, y = Inf, label = label),
            inherit.aes = FALSE,
            vjust = 1.2,
            size = 1.5) +
  facet_wrap(. ~ metric, scales = "free_y") +
  theme_bw() +
  xlab("date") +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("bluesky"="blue","x"="red")) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1, size = 8))
  
```

Wow, this is interesting. With 133 posts in both platform, we observed that the like counts on the day0 and day 4, were significantly more on X than on Bluesky! Should we be seeing more engagement on X if Bluesky is dead? 🤷‍♂️ Are Bluesky users the same as X users? Do they both behave the same in posting behavior? So many questions, so little data. But, one thing for sure, globally the post counts was the lowest since 2022. And for next year, it is a REALLY good idea to monitor both platforms because the good stuff is not all in one platform! Now if only I can get access to Roon's api... 🤔

<br>


## Limitations {#limit}
- Only looked at `idweek`, `idweek26`, `idweek2026` posts. Could very well miss those influential posts by reputable sources that do not include these tags.
- word cloud will miss pictures' texts. Some of the things I learnt were actually from snapshot of slides! Definitely a limitation
- we did not include mastodon


